1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,426
Friday deploy.

2
00:00:01,426 --> 00:00:03,675
The new code expects a new column.

3
00:00:03,675 --> 00:00:05,925
The database does not have it yet.

4
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:07,049
The site is down.

5
00:00:07,599 --> 00:00:11,139
Today you learn migrations: small numbered S Q L files,

6
00:00:11,139 --> 00:00:12,234
applied in order,

7
00:00:12,234 --> 00:00:13,135
recorded once,

8
00:00:13,135 --> 00:00:15,130
and rolled back when they fail.

9
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:18,404
You run a real runner on a real post gress.

10
00:00:18,404 --> 00:00:20,621
One migration will fail on purpose,

11
00:00:20,621 --> 00:00:21,888
and you will fix it.

12
00:00:22,438 --> 00:00:25,254
Change the database in small, recorded steps.

13
00:00:26,804 --> 00:00:29,876
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

14
00:00:30,426 --> 00:00:36,399
One: the words. Migration, version, the migrations table, transaction, and idempotent.

15
00:00:36,949 --> 00:00:42,389
Two: a live demo on real post gress. We run the runner, and one migration fails.

16
00:00:42,939 --> 00:00:47,846
Three: we see that the failed migration left nothing behind, fix it, and run again.

17
00:00:48,396 --> 00:00:50,194
Four: we add a new migration,

18
00:00:50,194 --> 00:00:52,674
and prove running twice changes nothing.

19
00:00:52,674 --> 00:00:53,604
Then your turn,

20
00:00:53,604 --> 00:00:56,332
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

21
00:00:56,882 --> 00:00:59,527
This is lesson four of the databases domain.

22
00:01:00,077 --> 00:01:02,893
Change the database in small, recorded steps.

23
00:01:04,943 --> 00:01:07,055
Six ideas for safe migrations.

24
00:01:07,605 --> 00:01:11,520
A migration is one S Q L file that changes the schema.

25
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,550
Name it with a number first,

26
00:01:13,550 --> 00:01:16,233
so the order is clear: zero zero one,

27
00:01:16,233 --> 00:01:17,248
zero zero two.

28
00:01:17,798 --> 00:01:20,215
The database remembers which ones ran,

29
00:01:20,215 --> 00:01:22,506
in a table called schema migrations.

30
00:01:22,506 --> 00:01:25,051
The runner only applies what is missing.

31
00:01:25,601 --> 00:01:28,289
Each migration runs in one transaction.

32
00:01:28,289 --> 00:01:29,874
If any statement fails,

33
00:01:29,874 --> 00:01:32,562
everything in that file is rolled back.

34
00:01:32,562 --> 00:01:33,665
No half changes.

35
00:01:34,215 --> 00:01:37,690
Idempotent means running the runner twice is safe.

36
00:01:37,690 --> 00:01:40,122
The second run finds nothing to do,

37
00:01:40,122 --> 00:01:40,956
and says so.

38
00:01:41,506 --> 00:01:44,871
Never edit a migration that has already been applied.

39
00:01:44,871 --> 00:01:47,665
Other databases already ran the old version.

40
00:01:47,665 --> 00:01:49,442
Add a new migration instead.

41
00:01:49,992 --> 00:01:51,002
In a deployment,

42
00:01:51,002 --> 00:01:53,715
the runner goes before the new code starts.

43
00:01:53,715 --> 00:01:54,473
If it fails,

44
00:01:54,473 --> 00:01:55,545
the deploy stops,

45
00:01:55,545 --> 00:01:57,502
and the old code keeps running.

46
00:01:59,052 --> 00:02:04,236
Go to the lab folder.

47
00:02:05,786 --> 00:02:07,685
Look around. Type: L S.

48
00:02:08,235 --> 00:02:13,931
A migrations folder, the runner called migrate dot S H, a data folder, and a task file.

49
00:02:14,481 --> 00:02:17,787
List the migrations.

50
00:02:18,337 --> 00:02:19,254
Three files.

51
00:02:19,254 --> 00:02:21,088
Zero zero one customers,

52
00:02:21,088 --> 00:02:22,615
zero zero two email,

53
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:24,372
zero zero three orders.

54
00:02:24,372 --> 00:02:26,359
The numbers set the order.

55
00:02:26,909 --> 00:02:33,607
Read one.

56
00:02:34,157 --> 00:02:35,140
Two statements.

57
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:36,450
Add an email column,

58
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:39,200
and fill it in for the existing customers.

59
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:39,855
One small,

60
00:02:39,855 --> 00:02:40,707
clear change.

61
00:02:41,257 --> 00:02:46,163
What is pending?

62
00:02:46,713 --> 00:02:49,231
All three are pending. Nothing has run yet.

63
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:53,343
Run the runner.

64
00:02:53,893 --> 00:02:55,532
Zero zero one applied.

65
00:02:55,532 --> 00:02:57,171
Zero zero two applied.

66
00:02:57,171 --> 00:03:02,013
Then zero zero three fails: the relation customer does not exist.

67
00:03:02,013 --> 00:03:03,726
The runner says FAILED,

68
00:03:03,726 --> 00:03:04,620
rolled back,

69
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:05,365
and stops.

70
00:03:05,365 --> 00:03:07,376
The deploy would stop here.

71
00:03:07,926 --> 00:03:14,582
What did the failure leave?

72
00:03:15,132 --> 00:03:15,808
Customers,

73
00:03:15,808 --> 00:03:17,972
and the schema migrations table.

74
00:03:17,972 --> 00:03:19,054
No orders table.

75
00:03:19,054 --> 00:03:21,691
The transaction rolled everything back.

76
00:03:21,691 --> 00:03:22,705
No half change.

77
00:03:23,255 --> 00:03:27,522
Check the status.

78
00:03:28,072 --> 00:03:31,720
Two applied, one pending. That is your first lab answer: two.

79
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:38,947
Write the answer.

80
00:03:39,497 --> 00:03:53,151
Fix the migration.

81
00:03:53,701 --> 00:03:58,565
Line three. The reference says customer. Change it to customers, with an S. Save.

82
00:04:09,615 --> 00:04:13,817
Run it again.

83
00:04:14,367 --> 00:04:16,223
Only zero zero three applies.

84
00:04:16,223 --> 00:04:17,887
The first two are skipped,

85
00:04:17,887 --> 00:04:20,063
because they are already recorded.

86
00:04:20,063 --> 00:04:20,383
Done,

87
00:04:20,383 --> 00:04:21,151
one applied.

88
00:04:21,701 --> 00:04:30,363
Write a new migration.

89
00:04:30,913 --> 00:04:32,906
Add a status column to orders,

90
00:04:32,906 --> 00:04:34,368
with a default of new.

91
00:04:34,368 --> 00:04:35,232
A new number,

92
00:04:35,232 --> 00:04:36,229
zero zero four.

93
00:04:36,229 --> 00:04:38,090
We never touch the old ones.

94
00:04:38,090 --> 00:04:38,422
Save.

95
00:04:45,072 --> 00:04:48,699
Apply it.

96
00:04:49,249 --> 00:04:51,873
Zero zero four applied. One applied.

97
00:04:52,423 --> 00:04:58,204
Run it again.

98
00:04:58,754 --> 00:05:03,447
Done, zero applied. Nothing to do, nothing changed. Running twice is safe.

99
00:05:03,997 --> 00:05:14,109
The history.

100
00:05:14,659 --> 00:05:17,347
Four versions, each with the time it was applied.

101
00:05:17,897 --> 00:05:27,903
The new column.

102
00:05:28,453 --> 00:05:31,123
The orders from zero zero three are there,

103
00:05:31,123 --> 00:05:33,221
and every one has the new status,

104
00:05:33,221 --> 00:05:33,476
new.

105
00:05:33,476 --> 00:05:34,938
The old data is intact.

106
00:05:35,488 --> 00:05:37,856
Run the judge.

107
00:05:38,406 --> 00:05:44,315
Five checks. Counted, fixed, new column, four versions, and data intact. Five out of five.

108
00:05:44,865 --> 00:05:46,828
Clean up.

109
00:05:47,378 --> 00:05:50,045
The server is stopped and everything is removed.

110
00:05:50,595 --> 00:05:59,341
Is it gone?

111
00:05:59,891 --> 00:06:02,409
No response. Nothing is left behind.

112
00:06:03,959 --> 00:06:07,906
Your task. Run the migrations, survive a failure, and add your own.

113
00:06:08,456 --> 00:06:09,811
One: run the runner.

114
00:06:09,811 --> 00:06:12,182
Two migrations apply and one fails.

115
00:06:12,182 --> 00:06:15,773
Write how many are applied now into answers dot text.

116
00:06:16,323 --> 00:06:21,528
Two: read the error, and fix migration zero zero three, which has never been applied.

117
00:06:22,078 --> 00:06:25,620
Three: run the runner again, so zero zero three applies.

118
00:06:26,170 --> 00:06:28,690
Four: add migration zero zero four,

119
00:06:28,690 --> 00:06:31,354
which adds a status column to orders.

120
00:06:31,354 --> 00:06:32,722
Five: run it twice,

121
00:06:32,722 --> 00:06:33,802
then lab check,

122
00:06:33,802 --> 00:06:34,810
then lab down.

123
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:40,636
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

124
00:06:41,186 --> 00:06:42,834
The lab lives on GitHub,

125
00:06:42,834 --> 00:06:44,001
at it dash waves,

126
00:06:44,001 --> 00:06:45,581
slash dev ops dash A I,

127
00:06:45,581 --> 00:06:47,091
in the folder academy,

128
00:06:47,091 --> 00:06:47,778
slash lab.

129
00:06:48,328 --> 00:06:54,003
Two files: lab one twenty-nine dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-nine dot S H.

130
00:06:54,553 --> 00:06:55,453
Install it,

131
00:06:55,453 --> 00:06:58,319
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

132
00:06:58,319 --> 00:07:00,203
dash m seven five five,

133
00:07:00,203 --> 00:07:02,495
lab one twenty-nine dot S H,

134
00:07:02,495 --> 00:07:03,723
into slash usr,

135
00:07:03,723 --> 00:07:04,706
slash local,

136
00:07:04,706 --> 00:07:05,689
slash S bin.

137
00:07:06,239 --> 00:07:11,012
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-nine dot S H.

138
00:07:11,012 --> 00:07:12,205
You get two: lab,

139
00:07:12,205 --> 00:07:12,767
and P G.

140
00:07:13,317 --> 00:07:14,947
You need post gress Q L,

141
00:07:14,947 --> 00:07:16,170
server and client,

142
00:07:16,170 --> 00:07:16,850
installed,

143
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:19,296
and port fifty-four ninety-one free.

144
00:07:19,296 --> 00:07:22,014
The lab runs its own private post gress,

145
00:07:22,014 --> 00:07:23,237
in its own folder.

146
00:07:23,787 --> 00:07:25,494
Lab up prepares everything.

147
00:07:25,494 --> 00:07:27,201
Lab check judges your work.

148
00:07:27,201 --> 00:07:28,718
Lab down removes it all.

149
00:07:28,718 --> 00:07:30,741
Only run it on your own machine.

150
00:07:32,791 --> 00:07:34,860
Pass or fail. You need all five.

151
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:38,440
One: you counted the two migrations that applied first.

152
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:42,510
Two: migration zero zero three is fixed and applied.

153
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:47,156
Three: your migration zero zero four added the status column.

154
00:07:47,706 --> 00:07:51,204
Four: the migrations table lists exactly four versions.

155
00:07:51,754 --> 00:07:53,930
Five: the customers kept their data.

156
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:55,782
To pass, all five.

157
00:07:57,832 --> 00:07:59,603
Your turn, on your own machine.

158
00:08:00,153 --> 00:08:02,691
One: lab up, run the runner, and write the answer.

159
00:08:03,241 --> 00:08:06,164
Two: read the error, and fix zero zero three.

160
00:08:06,714 --> 00:08:08,314
Three: run the runner again.

161
00:08:08,864 --> 00:08:11,851
Four: write zero zero four, and run it twice.

162
00:08:12,401 --> 00:08:16,219
Five: lab check until you see five out of five. Then lab down.

163
00:08:16,769 --> 00:08:18,604
Pause the video, and begin now.

164
00:08:34,154 --> 00:08:35,029
Quick check.

165
00:08:35,579 --> 00:08:37,477
Why are migration files numbered?

166
00:08:38,027 --> 00:08:41,163
What happened to the failed migration's changes, and why?

167
00:08:41,713 --> 00:08:44,209
Why never edit a migration that already ran?

168
00:08:44,759 --> 00:08:47,021
What does idempotent mean for the runner?

169
00:08:47,571 --> 00:08:50,008
Objectives covered: M C fourteen,

170
00:08:50,008 --> 00:08:51,115
objective five.

171
00:08:51,115 --> 00:08:54,291
Schema migrations in a deployment workflow.

172
00:08:54,841 --> 00:08:56,910
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

